		<h1 class="largetext">Rodcetnife.org Forums</h1>
		<h2 class="normaltext">General Category => Non-EQ (Hey Real Life!) => Topic started by: Rednight on March 18, 2004, 09:24:00 am</h2>

		<table width="90%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0">
			<tr>
				<td>
					<br />
					<hr size="2" width="100%" />
					Title: <b>Seattle Bands</b><br />
					Post by: <b>Rednight</b> on <b>March 18, 2004, 09:24:00 am</b>
					<hr />
					<div style="margin: 0 5ex;">Top 3 Bands that came out of Seattle in the 90s?<br /><br /></div>
					<br />
					<hr size="2" width="100%" />
					Title: <b>Re:Seattle Bands</b><br />
					Post by: <b>Kweil</b> on <b>March 18, 2004, 10:01:34 am</b>
					<hr />
					<div style="margin: 0 5ex;">Crappy_Band_1, Crappy_Band_2, and Crappy_Band_3</div>
					<br />
					<hr size="2" width="100%" />
					Title: <b>Re:Seattle Bands</b><br />
					Post by: <b>Callon</b> on <b>March 18, 2004, 02:22:27 pm</b>
					<hr />
					<div style="margin: 0 5ex;">The most influential were of course the bands that started the grunge movement.  They get a lot of the credit for popularizing the modern rock sound.  The biggest 3 from Seattle:<br /><br />Pearl Jam - Alive, Even Flow, and Jeremy all on the same album.   ;D<br />Nirvana, though I never cared for them much<br />eh... Soundgarden... i guess.   :P  </div>
					<br />
					<hr size="2" width="100%" />
					Title: <b>Re:Seattle Bands</b><br />
					Post by: <b>Kweil</b> on <b>March 18, 2004, 07:04:08 pm</b>
					<hr />
					<div style="margin: 0 5ex;">Pearl Jam and Nirvana came out before 1990.<br /><br />I honestly can&#039;t say I know of a good popular band that was formed in the last 15 years.</div>
					<br />
					<hr size="2" width="100%" />
					Title: <b>Re:Seattle Bands</b><br />
					Post by: <b>Givins</b> on <b>March 18, 2004, 10:23:32 pm</b>
					<hr />
					<div style="margin: 0 5ex;">Alice in Chains</div>
					<br />
					<hr size="2" width="100%" />
					Title: <b>Re:Seattle Bands</b><br />
					Post by: <b>Kweil</b> on <b>March 18, 2004, 10:26:14 pm</b>
					<hr />
					<div style="margin: 0 5ex;">I said good ;)</div>
					<br />
					<hr size="2" width="100%" />
					Title: <b>Re:Seattle Bands</b><br />
					Post by: <b>Gabus</b> on <b>March 19, 2004, 09:11:15 am</b>
					<hr />
					<div style="margin: 0 5ex;">Stone Temple Pilots? They hit in the 90&#039;s and were from Seattle werent they? (not that I particularly cared for them) Id have to say that Soundgarden and Alice in Chains were very good from that era and location as well. </div>
					<br />
					<hr size="2" width="100%" />
					Title: <b>Re:Seattle Bands</b><br />
					Post by: <b>Callon</b> on <b>March 19, 2004, 10:52:51 am</b>
					<hr />
					<div style="margin: 0 5ex;">pearl jam and nirvana may have existed before the &#039;90s, but virtually no one had heard of them.  The albums &quot;Ten&quot; and &quot;Nevermind&quot; both came out in &#039;91.  </div>
					<br />
					<hr size="2" width="100%" />
					Title: <b>Re:Seattle Bands</b><br />
					Post by: <b>EQSysquella</b> on <b>March 20, 2004, 11:34:13 pm</b>
					<hr />
					<div style="margin: 0 5ex;">Sir Mix-A-Lot was from Seattle ;) BABY GOT BACK.. omg so powa<br /><br />of course Nirvana (The Revolutionary band.. /bow)<br /><br />I can&#039;t remember, but I think Soundgarden is from Seattle (it is either Seattle or Chicago)..<br /><br /></div>
					<br />
					<hr size="2" width="100%" />
					Title: <b>Re:Seattle Bands</b><br />
					Post by: <b>Faelin</b> on <b>March 23, 2004, 07:25:28 pm</b>
					<hr />
					<div style="margin: 0 5ex;">Ahh a thread I can comment on!<br /><br />The band Mother Love Bone created grunge in the late, late 80s, but it was the heroin overdose death of it&#039;s lead singer Andy Wood that actually got the &quot;grunge&quot; sound moving along, as 2 of that band&#039;s members then joined what became Pearl Jam. The &quot;Temple of the Dog&quot; album was a tribute to him, which featured Soundgarden and Pearl Jam members to name a few. <br /><br />Nirvana (/bow) was purely Kurt&#039;s band, and a stellar band as well. Met the same fate as Mother Love Bone though.... heroin.<br /><br />Stone Temple Pilots aren&#039;t from Seattle - but were a good band too... also with heroin troubles. Hmm.<br /><br />Alice in Chains is another Seattle band with a sound I love... but damn that pesky heroin again! <br /><br />I was a huge fan of that sound ...but then again anything that got the glam-rock-crap band sound from good old Poison, Winger, Ratt, Night Ranger and Lita Ford OFF my radio, or any other sound eminating gadget I owned was a relief to say the least.<br /><br />Top 3 err 4?<br />1. Pearl Jam<br />2. Nirvana<br />3. Alice in Chains tied with Soundgarden.</div>
					<br />
					<hr size="2" width="100%" />
					Title: <b>Re:Seattle Bands</b><br />
					Post by: <b>Gabus</b> on <b>March 24, 2004, 08:30:05 am</b>
					<hr />
					<div style="margin: 0 5ex;"><div class="quoteheader">Quote from: Faelin $txt[176] March 23, 2004, 07:25:28 pm</div><div class="quote">Stone Temple Pilots aren&#039;t from Seattle - but were a good band too... also with heroin troubles. Hmm.<br /><br />Alice in Chains is another Seattle band with a sound I love... but damn that pesky heroin again! <br /><br />I was a huge fan of that sound ...but then again anything that got the glam-rock-crap band sound from good old Poison, Winger, Ratt, Night Ranger and Lita Ford OFF my radio, or any other sound eminating gadget I owned was a relief to say the least.<br /><br />Top 3 err 4?<br />1. Pearl Jam<br />2. Nirvana<br />3. Alice in Chains tied with Soundgarden.<br /></div><br /><br />Alice in Chains wasnt from Seattle? Hmm.. where were they from?<br /><br />I have that Temple of the Dog album on cassette somewhere. Great album.<br /><br />I was a HUGE headbanger in the 80&#039;s and 90&#039;s. I loved all those hair bands, and probably hung onto my mullet, acid washed jean jacket, and trans-am, much longer than I should have. :)</div>
					<br />
					<hr size="2" width="100%" />
					Title: <b>Re:Seattle Bands</b><br />
					Post by: <b>Rednight</b> on <b>March 24, 2004, 09:46:20 am</b>
					<hr />
					<div style="margin: 0 5ex;">Soundgarden<br />Nirvana<br />AnC<br />STP<br /><br />90&#039;s were some fun times  8)</div>
					<br />
					<hr size="2" width="100%" />
					Title: <b>Re:Seattle Bands</b><br />
					Post by: <b>Faelin</b> on <b>March 24, 2004, 10:48:38 am</b>
					<hr />
					<div style="margin: 0 5ex;">Yes Gabus, AiC was from Seattle. Stone Temple Pilots were from L.A. I tink you got dem mixed up in your quote of me.<br /><br />/cheers Red, putting Soundgarden first! /salute<br /><br />I think I could rank them in any order personally, cause I still love PJ and SG - and now that Cornell is with the old rage guys I like Audioslave&#039;s sound too.<br /><br />90&#039;s were some fun times indeed!</div>
					<br />
					<hr size="2" width="100%" />
					Title: <b>Re:Seattle Bands</b><br />
					Post by: <b>Callon</b> on <b>March 24, 2004, 10:59:22 am</b>
					<hr />
					<div style="margin: 0 5ex;"><div class="smalltext">Quote:</div><div class="quote">but then again anything that got the glam-rock-crap band sound from good old Poison, Winger, Ratt, Night Ranger and Lita Ford OFF my radio, or any other sound eminating gadget I owned was a relief to say the least.</div><br /><br />/cheer... I think that&#039;s partly why I loved the introduction of modern rock, too.  It displaced that damn glam rock crap.  <br /><br />The sad thing is how backwards my hometown is now.  Well, it&#039;s always been backwards, but during the &#039;90s we actually had a good modern rock radio station.  No glam rock (aka butt rock)!  Now they&#039;ve changed their format to be just like another rock station in town.  They play some modern rock and some butt rock... way too much butt rock.  Motley Crue, Metallica, Van Halen, etc., etc.  And an assload of Ozzy.... damn... Ozzy&#039;s not as bad as most of them, but they play him constantly.  How many times do I need to hear &quot;Bark at the Moon&quot; when I&#039;m driving to work in the morning?<br /><br />But then I live in a city that gets excited when Poison comes to town.  sigh....<br /><br />And what&#039;s with every damn dance club around playing nothing but hip hop now?  (unless it&#039;s a retro old people&#039;s place)  I&#039;m a techno/trance dork, but no one plays it.  :P<br /><br /></div>
					<br />
					<hr size="2" width="100%" />
					Title: <b>Re:Seattle Bands</b><br />
					Post by: <b>Rednight</b> on <b>March 24, 2004, 03:14:51 pm</b>
					<hr />
					<div style="margin: 0 5ex;">Heh hair bands....During the time GNR , Skid Row, Poison, Motley, etc....wearing flannel/acid washed jeans with combat boots....If I would of seen myself from back in the day, I would beat myself up =).<br /><br /><br /><br /><br /></div>
					<br />
					<hr size="2" width="100%" />
					Title: <b>Re:Seattle Bands</b><br />
					Post by: <b>Gamehenge</b> on <b>April 09, 2004, 04:10:37 am</b>
					<hr />
					<div style="margin: 0 5ex;">Pearl Jam was definately the only band to come out of seattle in the 90&#039;s with true skills in songwriting, emotion, and power.  They have a totally unique style, wich is rare, and a wide variety of musical worlds wich is pretty much unheard of in popular music.  Got to at least give em props for being themselves.  Wasn&#039;t Fugazi from Seattle?  They rocked!</div>
					<br /><br />
